.TH "ELEMENT.POINTERENTER_EVENT" "3JS" "July 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.pointerenter_event \- Element: pointerenter event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]pointerenter\f[R] event fires when a pointing device is moved
into the hit test boundaries of an element or one of its descendants,
including as a result of a \f[CR]pointerdown\f[R] event from a device
that does not support hover (see \f[CR]pointerdown\f[R]).
Otherwise, \f[CR]pointerenter\f[R] works the same as
\f[CR]mouseenter\f[R], and are dispatched at the same time.
They are also dispatched at the same time as \f[CR]mouseover\f[R] and
\f[CR]pointerover\f[R] events, if appropriate.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqpointerenter\(dq, (event) => { })

onpointerenter = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]PointerEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Using \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.addEventListener(\(dqpointerenter\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer entered element\(dq);
});
.EE
.PP
Using the \f[CR]onpointerenter\f[R] event handler property:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.onpointerenter = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer entered element\(dq);
};
.EE
